# name: test/sql/ast_unparse_leaf_text.test
# description: unparse leaf-text coverage - no leaf may unparse to its own node type (tracker 047 "4a")
# group: [sql]
#
# What this guards
# ----------------
# ast_unparse emits exactly one token per leaf node, as
#
#     COALESCE(NULLIF(name, ''), type)   over   WHERE children_count = 0
#
# (see src/sql_macros/ast_unparse.sql). So when a leaf's name-extraction
# strategy yields nothing, the unparser emits the node's *type* where its
# *text* belongs. For an anonymous token that is usually harmless, because
# tree-sitter names anonymous tokens after their own text. For a named one it
# is a round-trip correctness failure that is not flagged as lossy:
#
#     SELECT source FROM ast_unparse_code('# hello there' || chr(10) || 'x = 1', 'python');
#     -- emitted the literal string "comment" in place of "# hello there"
#
# The invariant below is the general form of that bug. Rather than enumerate
# "tokens that must not equal their type" (which is unfalsifiable for
# self-describing nodes like python's `true`, whose type legitimately IS its
# text), it asserts the stronger and exact property:
#
#     every leaf's emitted token equals that leaf's own source text
#
# `peek` is a sound independent oracle here precisely because the unparser
# deliberately never reads it - ast_unparse.sql "reconstructs source from AST
# rows WITHOUT peek". peek_mode := 'full' is required: the default truncates
# around 80 characters, which would make long comments look like mismatches.
#
# The gap list these tests were built from is derived from the grammars, not
# hand-written, by scripts/audit_leaf_text_gaps.py
# (tracker 047 requires this; see issue #184 for why a hand-enumerated list is
# not acceptable). Regenerate it after a grammar bump.
#
# Known limitations, deliberately NOT asserted here
# -------------------------------------------------
# * Byte-identity against the original file does not hold for any language in
#   this corpus. The unparser normalizes inter-token whitespace and does not
#   read start_column/end_column, so reconstructing exact gaps is the open
#   design question 041 records and 047 defers to "4b" (node templates).
# * Some grammars hide delimiter tokens entirely - tree-sitter-kotlin's
#   `string_literal` has no child node for its quote characters - so leaf
#   concatenation structurally cannot recover them. That is a 4b concern, not
#   a leaf-text one.
# * language 'duckdb' (the native, non-tree-sitter parser) is excluded. When
#   this file was written its adapter reported children_count = 0 for *every*
#   node, so the whole tree looked like leaves (#197); PR #205 fixed that, and
#   the tree structure is now correct. It stays excluded for the reason that
#   outlives the fix: its tree is an AST rather than a CST, with no node for any
#   keyword, punctuation or comment, so there is no leaf whose text could be
#   compared against its own source in the first place. See
#   test/sql/ast_unparse_exact.test for the re-measurement.

require sitting_duck

statement ok
LOAD sitting_duck;

# =============================================================================
# 1. The tracker 047 reproducer
# =============================================================================

# Python's `comment` had no name strategy, so its text was replaced by the
# literal word "comment".
query T
SELECT replace(source, chr(10), '|') FROM ast_unparse_code('# hello there' || chr(10) || 'x = 1', 'python');
----
# hello there|x = 1

# =============================================================================
# 2. The leaf-text invariant, per language
# =============================================================================
# Each file is a small valid program exercising comments, string bodies and
# escapes, and numeric literals - the node classes that carry text in a named
# leaf. Zero means every leaf would unparse to its own text.

# bash
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM read_ast('test/data/unparse_leaf_text/sample.sh', peek_mode := 'full')
WHERE children_count = 0 AND COALESCE(NULLIF(name, ''), type) <> peek;
----
0

